# Customer Support Ticket Classification System

# 1. Introduction

Customer support teams receive many tickets every day. Manually reading and sorting these tickets takes a lot of time and can delay important issues.

This project builds a Machine Learning system that can automatically:

* classify support tickets into categories
* assign priority levels

The goal is to reduce manual work and improve customer support efficiency.

---

# 2. Dataset Used

The dataset contains around **8,469 customer support tickets**.

Each ticket includes:

* Ticket subject
* Ticket description
* Ticket type
* Ticket priority
* Customer details
* Support channel

### Ticket Categories

* Refund Request
* Technical Issue
* Cancellation Request
* Product Inquiry
* Billing Inquiry

### Priority Levels

* Critical
* High
* Medium
* Low

The dataset was balanced, meaning all categories had similar numbers of records.

---

# 3. Data Preprocessing

Before training the model, the text data was cleaned.

The following preprocessing steps were applied:

* converted text to lowercase
* removed punctuation and special characters
* removed URLs and emails
* removed extra spaces

The ticket subject and description were combined into one text field for better prediction.

---

# 4. Feature Engineering

The text data was converted into numerical form using **TF-IDF Vectorization**.

TF-IDF helps identify important words in tickets.

Examples:

* refund
* payment
* network
* billing

These words help the model understand ticket categories.

---

# 5. Models Used

Three Machine Learning algorithms were tested:

| Model               | Purpose                 |
| ------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Logistic Regression | Text classification     |
| Naive Bayes         | Fast NLP classification |
| Random Forest       | Ensemble learning       |

Separate models were trained for:

* Ticket Type Prediction
* Priority Prediction

---

# 6. Results

## Ticket Type Classification

| Model               | Accuracy |
| ------------------- | -------- |
| Logistic Regression | ~32%     |
| Naive Bayes         | ~32%     |
| Random Forest       | ~22%     |

Logistic Regression performed best.

---

## Priority Prediction

| Model               | Accuracy |
| ------------------- | -------- |
| Logistic Regression | ~26%     |
| Naive Bayes         | ~26%     |
| Random Forest       | ~25%     |

Priority prediction was difficult because priority often depends on factors beyond text.

---

# 7. Analysis

The system was able to identify useful patterns from customer tickets.

However:

* many tickets contained repetitive template text
* priority labels were influenced by non-text information

Even with moderate accuracy, the system can still help reduce manual ticket sorting.

---

